member.remarks:
This method is needed for targetting both .NET Framework and .NET Core projects together. It is not needed for .NET Core only projects, that already natively support checking if the methods is defined using generics. Projects that support .NET Framework in addition do not have this and using the non-generic versions result in the IDE warning CA2263.
Normally, code in .NET Framework would look like:
if (!Enum.IsDefined(typeof(ConsoleColor), Console.ForegroundColor)) return false;
This results in the warning CA2263 on .NET Core, and is resolved with the following code (that uses generics) which does not compile on .NET Framework:
if (!Enum.IsDefined(Console.ForegroundColor)) return false;
The solution is to use this method in the form:
if (!Console.ForegroundColor.IsDefined()) return false;
An alternative way to drop in replacement for these methods is to use type aliasing in .NET Framework projects:
#if NETFRAMEWORK using Enum = RJCP.Core.EnumExt; #endif
if (!Enum.IsDefined(Console.ForegroundColor)) return false;

This method is needed for targetting both .NET Framework and .NET Core projects together. It is not needed for .NET Core only projects, that already natively support checking if the methods is defined using generics. Projects that support .NET Framework in addition do not have this and using the non-generic versions result in the IDE warning CA2263.
Normally, code in .NET Framework would look like:
foreach (var x in Enum.GetValues(typeof(ConsoleColor))) { ... }
This results in the warning CA2263 on .NET Core, and is resolved with the following code (that uses generics) which does not compile on .NET Framework:
foreach (var x in Enum.GetValues<ConsoleColor>()) { ... }
The solution is to use this method in the form:
foreach (var x in EnumExt.GetValues<ConsoleColor>()) { ... }
An alternative way to drop in replacement for these methods is to use type aliasing in .NET Framework projects:
#if NETFRAMEWORK using Enum = RJCP.Core.EnumExt; #endif
var valuesExt = Enum.GetValues<ConsoleColor>();

This method is needed for targetting both .NET Framework and .NET Core projects together. It is not needed for .NET Core only projects, that already natively support checking if the methods is defined using generics. Projects that support .NET Framework in addition do not have this and using the non-generic versions result in the IDE warning CA2263.
Normally, code in .NET Framework would look like:
ConsoleColor color = (ConsoleColor)Enum.Parse(typeof(ConsoleColor), "Red", true);
This results in the warning CA2263 on .NET Core, and is resolved with the following code (that uses generics) which does not compile on .NET Framework:
ConsoleColor color = Enum.Parse<ConsoleColor>("Red", true);
The solution is to use this method in the form:
ConsoleColor color = EnumExt.Parse<ConsoleColor>("Red", true);
An alternative way to drop in replacement for these methods is to use type aliasing in .NET Framework projects:
#if NETFRAMEWORK using Enum = RJCP.Core.EnumExt; #endif
ConsoleColor color = Enum.Parse<ConsoleColor>("Red", true);

